"""Reviewed stimuli and credit allocations responding to 29 October feedback."""
CHANGES={}
def add(k,p,a,g,ao=None,subtype=None,bloom='Apply',recall=0):
 CHANGES[k]={'prompt':p,'canonical_answer':a,'marking_guidance':g.split('|'),'ao':ao,'subtype':subtype,'blooms_level':bloom,'recall':recall}
# Topic 1 foundation skills: now include correction, selection or a second operation.
add('1-001','Calculate the gas volume retained in a syringe. Its initial reading is 7.0 cm³ and final reading 34.0 cm³; 3.0 cm³ of the increase is deliberately injected air, not reaction gas.','Reaction gas volume = (34.0 − 7.0) − 3.0 = 24.0 cm³.','Subtract initial reading and injected air: (34.0 − 7.0) − 3.0 (1).|24.0 cm³ reaction gas (1).')
add('1-002','Calculate the average gas-production rate between readings at 4 s and 18 s. The syringe reads 6 cm³ at 4 s and 48 cm³ at 18 s.','Change = 42 cm³ in 14 s, so average rate = 3.0 cm³/s.','Use both differences: (48−6)/(18−4) (1).|3.0 cm³/s (1).')
add('1-003','Calculate the corrected mean of balance readings 18.8, 19.0 and 19.2 g when a calibration check establishes a constant +0.2 g offset.','Mean indicated mass = 19.0 g; corrected mean = 18.8 g.','Average the three readings and subtract the positive offset (1).|18.8 g (1).')
add('1-004','Calculate the percentage of pure salt in a sample of total mass 30.0 g containing 6.0 g insoluble impurity.','Pure salt mass = 24.0 g; fraction = 24.0/30.0 = 0.800, giving 80.0%.','Find pure mass by subtracting 6.0 g from 30.0 g (1).|80.0% from pure mass divided by total mass (1).')
add('1-005','Calculate the solute amount in the liquid left after 50 cm³ is removed from 200 cm³ of a uniform solution. Every 100 cm³ contains 0.020 mol; no solvent evaporates.','150 cm³ remains; solute amount = 0.020 × 150/100 = 0.030 mol.','150 cm³ remaining, then apply the direct-proportion relationship (1).|0.030 mol (1).')
add('1-006','Calculate the total time to transfer 60 cm³ at a constant flow of 5 cm³/s if the flow is stopped for 4 s halfway through. Volume = rate × flowing time.','Flowing time = 12 s; total elapsed time = 16 s.','60/5 gives 12 s flowing time (1).|Add the pause to give 16 s elapsed time (1).')
add('1-007','Calculate the total solute mass in 3.0 × 10² equal portions, each containing 2.0 × 10⁻³ g, after 0.10 g is lost in transfer.','Initial total = 0.60 g; recovered total = 0.50 g.','Multiply portions by mass per portion: 0.60 g (1).|Subtract the loss: 0.50 g (1).')
add('1-008','Calculate the concentration when 8.0 × 10⁻³ mol solute is dissolved in 200 cm³ solution. Express the concentration in mol/dm³.','200 cm³ = 0.200 dm³; concentration = 0.0080/0.200 = 0.040 mol/dm³.','Convert 200 cm³ to 0.200 dm³ (1).|0.040 mol/dm³ from amount divided by converted volume (1).')
add('1-009','Calculate an estimate of the material per portion if a balance reads 19.8 g including a 4.9 g container, and the material is divided into three equal portions. First round the readings to 20 g and 5 g.','Estimated material mass = 15 g; estimated mass per portion = 5 g.','Subtract rounded container mass from rounded total: 15 g (1).|Divide by three to obtain about 5 g per portion (1).')
add('1-010','Calculate an estimate of liquid mass remaining from 9.8 cm³ after 2.1 cm³ is poured away. Its density is 2.1 g/cm³. Round the volumes to 10 and 2 cm³ and density to 2 g/cm³.','About 8 cm³ remains; mass ≈ 8 × 2 = 16 g.','Remaining estimated volume 10−2 = 8 cm³ (1).|Approximately 16 g (1).')
add('1-011','Calculate the sample volume when a filled container has mass 51 g, the empty container 15 g, and sample density 3.0 g/cm³. Mass = density × volume.','Sample mass = 36 g; volume = 36/3.0 = 12 cm³.','Subtract container mass to obtain 36 g (1).|12 cm³ from sample mass divided by density (1).')
add('1-012','Calculate a solid sample’s density. Its mass is 45 g; water in a measuring cylinder rises from 20 to 35 cm³ when the insoluble sample is fully immersed.','Displaced volume = 15 cm³; density = 45/15 = 3.0 g/cm³.','Use the 15 cm³ rise as sample volume (1).|3.0 g/cm³ (1).')
add('1-032','Calculate the combined solution volume in dm³ when 175 cm³ is added to 0.100 dm³. Assume volumes are additive.','175 cm³ = 0.175 dm³; total = 0.275 dm³.','Convert 175 cm³ to 0.175 dm³ (1).|Add 0.100 dm³ to give 0.275 dm³ (1).')
add('1-033','Calculate the mass per sample in standard scientific notation if 0.00216 g is divided equally among three samples.','Mass per sample = 0.00072 g = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ g.','Divide total mass by three: 0.00072 g (1).|Correct standard form 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ g (1).')
add('1-034','Calculate the total volume in ordinary decimal notation after combining 2.40 × 10³ cm³ and 1.20 × 10³ cm³.','Total = 3.60 × 10³ cm³ = 3600 cm³.','Combine correctly to 3.60 × 10³ cm³ (1).|3600 cm³ in ordinary notation (1).')
add('1-038','Calculate the corrected Kelvin temperature when a thermometer reads 25.50 °C and calibration shows a +0.50 °C offset. T/K = T/°C + 273.15.','Corrected Celsius reading = 25.00 °C; temperature = 298.15 K.','Subtract the positive offset before converting (1).|298.15 K (1).')
add('1-039','Calculate the final Celsius temperature of a sample initially at 310.15 K that cools by 12.00 °C. T/K = T/°C + 273.15; one kelvin of temperature change equals one degree Celsius.','Initial temperature = 37.00 °C; final temperature = 25.00 °C.','Convert initial temperature to 37.00 °C (1).|Subtract the cooling change to obtain 25.00 °C (1).')
add('1-073','Calculate Rf from ruler heights measured above the paper bottom: baseline 1.0 cm, spot 4.6 cm, solvent front 7.0 cm. Rf uses distances measured from the baseline.','Spot travel = 3.6 cm and front travel = 6.0 cm; Rf = 0.60.','Subtract baseline height from both readings (1).|Rf = 3.6/6.0 = 0.60, no unit (1).')
add('1-074','Calculate a spot’s height above the paper bottom when its Rf is 0.40, the baseline is at 1.0 cm and the solvent front is at 8.5 cm.','Front travel = 7.5 cm; spot travel = 3.0 cm; spot height = 4.0 cm.','Correct baseline-referenced front and spot travel (1).|Add baseline height to obtain 4.0 cm (1).')
add('4-017','Calculate the gas volume remaining at room temperature and pressure after 0.050 mol is removed from 0.300 mol gas. Use 24 dm³/mol.','Amount remaining = 0.250 mol; volume = 6.00 dm³ at room temperature and pressure.','Subtract to obtain 0.250 mol (1).|6.00 dm³ at the stated room conditions (1).')
add('4-033','Calculate percentage yield when a container holding dry pure product has mass 10.00 g, its empty mass is 2.00 g, and the theoretical product mass is 10.0 g.','Product mass = 8.00 g; yield fraction = 0.800; percentage yield = 80.0%.','Subtract container mass to obtain 8.00 g product (1).|80.0% using product mass/theoretical mass (1).')
add('4-034','Calculate percentage yield when a product container has total mass 6.00 g, its empty mass is 1.24 g, and theoretical dry pure product mass is 5.60 g. The recovered product is dry and pure.','Product mass = 4.76 g; fraction = 4.76/5.60 = 0.850; percentage yield = 85.0%.','4.76 g product after tare subtraction (1).|85.0% (1).')
add('4-035','Calculate the theoretical product mass when a container plus dry pure product weighs 11.00 g, the empty container weighs 2.00 g, and the percentage yield is 75.0%.','Actual product = 9.00 g; theoretical mass = 9.00/0.750 = 12.0 g.','Subtract the tare and divide by the 0.750 yield fraction (1).|12.0 g theoretical product (1).')
add('4-037','Calculate percentage purity of a 15.0 g sample after analysis finds 3.0 g impurity and the remainder is the desired substance.','Pure mass = 12.0 g; fraction = 0.800; purity = 80.0%.','Subtract impurity to find 12.0 g pure substance (1).|80.0% (1).')
add('4-038','Calculate percentage purity when a container and sample weigh 7.50 g, the empty container weighs 2.50 g, and analysis finds 4.25 g desired pure substance.','Sample mass = 5.00 g; pure fraction = 4.25/5.00 = 0.850; purity = 85.0%.','Use 5.00 g sample after tare subtraction (1).|85.0% (1).')
add('4-039','Calculate the mass of pure substance when a container plus sample weighs 30.0 g, the empty container 10.0 g, and the sample is 90.0% pure by mass.','Sample mass = 20.0 g; pure mass = 20.0 × 0.900 = 18.0 g.','20.0 g sample after subtracting container (1).|18.0 g pure substance (1).')
# Address the cited weak explanations and eliminate the base/alkali duplication.
add('7-003','Explain why a soluble base produces a conducting alkaline solution, using its dissolved particles.','Dissolution provides mobile ions, including hydroxide ions; OH⁻ makes the solution alkaline while mobile ions carry current.','Mobile dissolved ions carry current and the presence of OH⁻ accounts for alkalinity; both links required for the single mark (1).',ao=['A'],subtype='MECH',bloom='Understand')
add('7-028','Explain why adding an insoluble basic oxide can raise the pH of an acidic solution even though the oxide is not an alkali.','The oxide reacts with and consumes H⁺ at its surface; lower hydrogen-ion concentration raises pH without requiring the oxide to dissolve as an alkali first.','Consumption of H⁺ lowers its concentration and therefore raises pH; both cause and consequence required (1).',ao=['A'],subtype='MECH',bloom='Understand')
add('7-024','Explain why the concentration halves when a sodium hydroxide solution is diluted to twice its original volume without losing solute.','The amount of solute remains unchanged while volume doubles; amount per volume therefore halves.','Unchanged solute amount divided by twice the volume gives half the concentration (1).',ao=['B'],subtype='APP')
add('9-048','Explain why scratching an aluminium surface can briefly increase reaction with air before the reaction slows again.','Scratching exposes metal by breaking the oxide barrier; new oxide forms on the exposed metal and again restricts reactant contact.','Barrier removal exposes reactive metal, followed by renewed oxide formation that limits contact; complete sequence required (1).',ao=['A'],subtype='MECH',bloom='Understand')
add('7-014','Suggest the gas expected when zinc reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid, using Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl2 + ____ to account for the remaining atoms.','H2: two hydrogen atoms remain after zinc and chlorine are assigned to ZnCl2.','H2 supported by conserving the two remaining hydrogen atoms (1).',ao=['B'],subtype='APP')
add('9-004','Identify the alloy diagram and justify the choice. Each symbol represents an atom in a solid metallic sample: ○ is one metallic element and ● a different one. Diagram A contains only ○; diagram B contains both ○ and ● mixed in one array.','B is the alloy because two different elements are mixed in the metallic structure; A shows only one element.','B together with the two-element evidence is required for one mark; B alone is insufficient (1).',ao=['B'],subtype='INTERP',bloom='Analyse')
# Rebalance existing factual pairs into two explicit causal links; keep AO A for
# familiar chemistry explanations and use B only for supplied-data decisions.
def why(k,p,a,g):add(k,p,a,g,ao=['A'],subtype='MECH',bloom='Understand')
why('2-133','Describe why positive ions in a metal do not simply repel apart, and why the metal can conduct while solid.','Delocalised electrons attract the positive ions and maintain bonding; those electrons can move through the solid and carry charge.','Ion–electron attraction opposes separation of the ions (1).|Mobile delocalised electrons carry current through the solid (1).')
why('2-135','Describe why a metal can be hammered into a new shape and remain bonded, whereas moving layers of an ionic crystal can cause fracture.','Metallic layers can shift while retaining attraction to delocalised electrons; shifted ionic layers can bring like charges together, causing repulsion and cleavage.','Maintained ion–electron attraction after metal layers shift (1).|Like-charge repulsion after ionic layers shift (1).')
why('9-033','Describe why recycling can slow depletion of an ore deposit but cannot make the deposit itself renewable on a human timescale.','Recovered metal replaces some demand for newly extracted ore; recycling does not rapidly regenerate the geological deposit.','Recovered metal reduces fresh extraction demand (1).|Geological deposit is not rapidly regenerated by recycling (1).')
why('9-039','Describe why increasing the carbon content of steel may improve resistance to scratching but make forming a thin curved panel harder.','Carbon disrupts layer movement, increasing hardness; reduced ease of deformation makes shaping more difficult.','Disrupted layer movement linked to greater hardness (1).|Reduced ductility linked to the forming difficulty (1).')
why('9-046','Describe why zinc-coated iron may resist rusting even after a small scratch, provided surrounding zinc remains in electrical contact in moist air.','Intact coating excludes reactants; at a scratch the more reactive zinc oxidises preferentially and protects exposed iron.','Intact barrier excludes water/oxygen (1).|Preferential zinc oxidation protects exposed iron under the stated contact conditions (1).')
why('10-006','Describe why fractional distillation separates petroleum without converting it into entirely new hydrocarbons.','Different components vaporise and condense at different temperatures; these are physical changes that preserve the molecules.','Different boiling behaviour separates fractions (1).|Phase changes preserve molecular identities rather than breaking molecules (1).')
why('10-015','Describe why a homologous series can show similar reactions while its boiling points change as molecules grow.','The shared functional group gives similar chemistry; larger molecules generally have stronger intermolecular attractions and need more energy to separate.','Common functional group linked to characteristic reactions (1).|Increasing intermolecular attraction linked to higher boiling temperature (1).')
why('10-019','Describe why a saturated open-chain hydrocarbon with three carbon atoms has eight hydrogen atoms when carbon makes four bonds and hydrogen one.','The two C–C single bonds use four carbon bonding positions in total; eight of the twelve positions remain for H.','Twelve carbon bonding positions minus four used by C–C links (1).|Eight remaining C–H bonds give C3H8 (1).')
why('10-023','Describe why CH4 + Cl2 → CH3Cl + HCl is substitution and why the equation cannot instead produce CH4Cl plus HCl.','One H on methane is replaced by Cl; the proposed alternative would exceed carbon valency and fail hydrogen balance.','Replacement of one H by Cl identifies substitution (1).|Proposed alternative violates carbon valency or hydrogen conservation (1).')
why('10-025','Describe why an open-chain hydrocarbon with one C=C bond has two fewer hydrogen atoms than the corresponding alkane with the same carbon count.','The additional C–C bond uses one further bonding position on each carbon involved, leaving two fewer positions for H.','Double bond uses two additional carbon bonding positions (1).|Two fewer C–H bonds explain the hydrogen difference (1).')
why('10-033','Describe why the OH in ethanol does not imply a supply of free hydroxide ions like that in aqueous sodium hydroxide.','Ethanol has a covalent O–H group within a neutral molecule; sodium hydroxide supplies mobile OH⁻ ions on dissolving.','Covalent neutral-molecule OH distinguished from OH⁻ (1).|Dissolved NaOH supplies hydroxide ions whereas that inference does not follow for ethanol (1).')
why('10-037','Describe why a well-aerated ethanol flame can heat a container while its collected gaseous products include both water vapour and CO2.','Product bond formation releases more energy than reactant bond breaking absorbs; oxidation transfers the fuel’s H and C into water and CO2.','Net bond-energy release accounts for heating (1).|Complete oxidation conserves H and C in water and CO2 (1).')
why('10-040','Describe why the group C(=O)–OH must be treated as a complete carboxyl group rather than identifying its OH as a separate alcohol group.','The OH is bonded to the carbonyl carbon; the combined connectivity gives carboxylic-acid behaviour rather than alcohol behaviour.','OH connection to carbonyl carbon identifies the whole group (1).|Combined group governs acid behaviour, so isolated OH lettering is insufficient (1).')
why('10-047','Describe why joining many small monomers can give a large polymer while leaving some structural features of each monomer recognisable.','New covalent links join the units into one extended molecule; portions not involved in forming those links remain in the repeat units.','New inter-unit bonds create one larger chain (1).|Unreacted portions of monomer structures remain within repeat units (1).')
why('10-049','Describe why condensation polymerisation can release water without violating conservation of mass.','Atoms removed from monomer functional groups form water; the mass of polymer plus eliminated water equals total reactant mass.','Eliminated H/O atoms accounted for in water (1).|Including water in total product mass preserves mass balance (1).')
why('10-062','Describe why finding amide links in a fibre is insufficient to identify it as a protein rather than nylon.','Both can contain the amide linkage; identifying their different monomer-derived units is also necessary.','Shared linkage makes the observation non-unique (1).|Different units must be identified to distinguish the polymers (1).')
why('10-068','Describe why a fat molecule and Terylene can both undergo ester-link hydrolysis without being the same kind of molecular structure.','Both contain ester links that can break on hydrolysis; their constituent units and arrangement differ, with Terylene forming repeating polymer chains.','Shared ester links account for hydrolysis (1).|Different units/chain arrangement explain distinct structures (1).')
why('10-080','Describe why breaking a discarded plastic bag into small fragments does not necessarily remove its environmental effects.','Fragmentation need not chemically biodegrade the polymer; persistent smaller pieces can disperse and be taken up by organisms.','Physical fragmentation distinguished from chemical biodegradation (1).|Persistence and wider dispersal/uptake explain continued harm (1).')
why('11-017','Describe why acid cleaning of calcium carbonate scale produces gas and reduces the solid deposit.','Acid reacts with carbonate to form CO2 and water; the calcium becomes part of a salt, removing the original insoluble scale.','Acid–carbonate reaction releases CO2 (1).|Conversion of the carbonate deposit to salt accounts for its loss (1).')
why('11-047','Describe why an engine can emit CO even with sulfur-free fuel, while SO2 formation requires a sulfur source.','Insufficient oxidation of fuel carbon can form CO; sulfur conservation means SO2 cannot form without sulfur in a reactant.','Incomplete carbon oxidation accounts for CO independently of sulfur (1).|SO2 requires a sulfur source by element conservation (1).')
